// WHAT THIS IS FOR: a premise that is itself supported by a further argument -- the case that
// decides whether "premises of one step fold and unfold as one" is a fold rule or a fold bug.
//
// Premises of one inference step assert nothing individually: knock one out and the step is
// destroyed, which is what distinguishes a LINKED step from a list of independent `+` reasons.
// So they fold as one -- there is no reader question to which "show me premise two but not
// premise one" is the answer.
//
// BUT A SUPPORTED PREMISE STILL UNFOLDS SEPARATELY. [Contested premise] below has an argument of
// its own standing behind it. That argument is A LEVEL DOWN, not a second premise of the step
// above, so revealing it is one click of the ordinary ladder and NOT a violation of
// "opening a section reveals one level of it, not several".
//
// The two rules pull in opposite directions and that is exactly why this file exists: fold the
// premises as one group, and the group's own badge must still descend into the supported
// premise one level at a time.
//
// WHAT TO SEE:
//   - the three premises of <Main Argument> fold and unfold together, as one enclosure
//   - [Contested premise] keeps a fold badge OF ITS OWN, standing for <Backing Argument>
//   - unfolding the premise group does NOT bring <Backing Argument> on screen with it
//   - unfolding [Contested premise] afterwards brings it, and only it
//
// IF THE FOLD RULE IS WRONG in the direction that costs most: opening the premise group reveals
// the backing argument too, which is the "several levels at once" defect KNOWN-ISSUES.md records
// being fixed once already.

[Contention]: The policy should be adopted.

<Main Argument>: The policy is both effective and affordable.

(1) [Effective]: The policy achieves what it sets out to achieve.
(2) [Contested premise]: The policy is affordable within the current budget.
(3) [Sufficient]: An effective and affordable policy should be adopted.
-- Modus ponens {uses: [1,2,3]} --
(4) [Adopt]: The policy should be adopted.
    +> [Contention]

<Backing Argument>: The costing has been independently audited.

(1) [Audit]: The independent audit put the cost below the budget line.
(2) [Audit reliable]: The auditor has no stake in the outcome.
-----
(3) [Costing sound]: The costing can be relied on.
    +> [Contested premise]
